Q: How does security compare (Spring Security vs Quarkus)?

You already know JWT filters + SecurityFilterChain in Spring.
Quarkus uses **extensions** + annotation/permission checks instead of Spring Security’s filter chain model.

────────────────────────────────────────
Common Quarkus options

1) OIDC (Keycloak / OAuth2 provider)
quarkus-oidc — validate bearer tokens from IdP

2) SmallRye JWT
Validate JWT locally with public key / secret

3) Role / permission annotations on resources
@RolesAllowed("admin")
on JAX-RS methods

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring mapping (conceptual)

| Spring Boot                         | Quarkus                          |
| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| spring-boot-starter-security        | quarkus-oidc / smallrye-jwt ...  |
| JwtAuthenticationFilter             | built-in token validation layers |
| SecurityFilterChain matchers        | proactive auth + annotations     |
| `@PreAuthorize` / `hasRole`         | `@RolesAllowed` / permissions    |
| Stateful session CSRF concerns      | Often bearer token APIs          |

────────────────────────────────────────
What to say in interview

“In Spring I implemented JWT with a filter and SecurityContext.
In Quarkus I’d use OIDC or SmallRye JWT extensions and secure JAX-RS endpoints with roles/permissions.
Flow is the same idea — validate token, establish identity, authorize endpoint — different APIs.”

Prepare to discuss:
- access token validation
- roles in claims
- public vs authenticated `@Path`s
- same CSRF rule: cookie sessions need CSRF; bearer header APIs usually don’t

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

“Quarkus security is extension-based (OIDC/JWT) with JAX-RS role checks; same authn/authz goals as Spring Security, less of the classic SecurityFilterChain style.”
